<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quảng Bình province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quảng Bình is a historical province of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, merged into Quảng Trị province in 2025. It marked the northern edge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>'s coastal lands until the Vietnamese gained the area in the 11th century, and in the 17th and 18th centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> defended it with walls against the Trịnh. It contains the Phong Nha-Kẻ Bàng caves.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quang-binh-province.jpg" alt="Quảng Bình province" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Genghiskhanviet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phongnhakebang7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quảng Bình lies on the narrow coast of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, with Hà Tĩnh to the north, Quảng Trị to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> to the west and the sea to the east. At its narrowest the province is only 40 kilometres from the sea to the Laotian border. Hills and mountains cover 85 per cent of it, and the Annamite Range forms the border with Laos, its highest peak, Phi Co Pi, rising to 2,017 metres. A belt of sand dunes along the coast acts as a natural dam against the tides. Most of its rivers rise in the Truong Son Range and flow to the sea, among them the Gianh and the Nhật Lệ.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations show that people lived in the area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>. In 1926 the French archaeologist Madeleine Colani excavated caves in the western mountains and assigned the finds to the Hòa Bình culture, with a radiocarbon date of 10,509 years ago, plus or minus 950. Some pottery from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Đông Sơn culture</a> was also found. Under the Hùng kings the area belonged to the Việt Thường group.</p><p>King Chế Củ of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> adopted a hostile policy toward the Vietnamese, and in 1068 he provoked a conflict. In February 1069 the fleet of King Lý Thánh Tông of Đại Việt scattered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> forces at the mouth of the Nhật Lệ River and took the Cham capital, Vijaya. Chế Củ was captured and secured his release only by ceding three districts, Bố Chính, Địa-ly and Ma-Linh, corresponding to present-day Quảng Bình and northern Quảng Trị. In 1469 the area became the Tân Bình prefecture, and in 1604 it was named Quảng Bình.</p><p>In the 17th century, during the Trịnh-Nguyễn war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gianh-river">Gianh River</a> became the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Đàng Trong</a> in the south and Đàng Ngoài in the north, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-hoi">Đồng Hới</a> was built as a fortress to protect the Nguyễn lords' territory. Quảng Bình became an important front. Under French rule it belonged to Annam. After the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> in 1954 it was the southernmost province of North Vietnam, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> it was the most heavily bombed province, by U.S. B-52s. In 1976 it was merged with Quảng Trị and Thừa Thiên into Bình Trị Thiên, and in 1990 the three were separated again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On 12 June 2025 Quảng Bình was dissolved and merged with Quảng Trị province. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-hoi">Đồng Hới</a>. The Phong Nha-Kẻ Bàng National Park, listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2003, protects a karst region with some 300 caves and grottoes, including the longest underground river, and Sơn Đoòng Cave is open only to limited guided tours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quảng Bình province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
